{"records":[{"id":"r3vwyr2w5x","title":"Direct detection of SARS-CoV-2 using CRISPR-Cas13a and a mobile phone-based device. Fozouni. et al.","description":"We report the development of an amplification-free CRISPR-Cas13a assay for direct detection of SARS-CoV-2 from nasal swab RNA that can be read with a mobile phone microscope. The assay achieved ~100 copies/uL sensitivity in under 30 minutes of measurement time  and accurately detected pre-extracted RNA from a set of positive clinical samples in under 5 minutes. We combined crRNAs targeting SARS-CoV-2 RNA to improve sensitivity and specificity, and directly quantified viral load using enzyme kinetics. Integrated with a reader device based on a mobile phone, this assay has the potential to enable rapid, low-cost, point-of-care screening for SARS-CoV-2.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-09T15:25:47.11Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r3vwyr2w5x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r3vwyr2w5x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Parinaz","last_name":"Fozouni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sungmin","last_name":"Son","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maria","last_name":"Diaz de Leon Derby","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gavin","last_name":"Knott","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carley","last_name":"Gray","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"D'Ambrosio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chunyu","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Neil","last_name":"Switz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Renuka","last_name":"Kumar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephanie","last_name":"Stephens","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniela","last_name":"Boehm","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chia-Lin","last_name":"Tsou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jeffrey","last_name":"Shu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Abdul","last_name":"Bhuiya","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Max","last_name":"Armstrong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Harris","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pei-Yi","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jeannette","last_name":"Osterloh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anke","last_name":"Meyer-Franke","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bastian","last_name":"Joehnk","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keith","last_name":"Walcott","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anita","last_name":"Sil","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charles","last_name":"Langelier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katherine","last_name":"Pollard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Emily","last_name":"Crawford","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andreas","last_name":"Puschnik","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maira","last_name":"Phelps","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amy","last_name":"Kistler","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joseph","last_name":"DeRisi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jennifer","last_name":"Doudna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel ","last_name":"Fletcher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melanie","last_name":"Ott","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Software","Image Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vnbpprr393","title":"A screen to identify targeted protein degraders discovers an induced proximity degrader of the oncoprotein SKP2","description":"Source data including the Masspec data, unprocessed western blot and gels, and statistical source data for \"A screen to identify targeted protein degraders discovers a degrader of the undruggable oncoprotein SKP2 \" published in Nature Biotechnology.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-13T07:54:43.125Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vnbpprr393","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vnbpprr393"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiang","last_name":"Hai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yankai","last_name":"Chu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences","Drug Discovery"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3zygrwcj4t","title":"The contribution of native protein complexes to targeted protein degradation","description":"Targeted protein degradation (TPD) destroys proteins of interest (POIs) by hijacking the cellular proteolytic machinery. Most proteins in cells exist and function as part of multi-protein or macromolecular complexes, thereby allowing a single protein to control multiple biological processes. Therefore, when a small molecule degrader induces proximity between an E3 ligase and the POI, the macromolecular context of the POI potentially influences the degradation outcomes of the POI and of the complex components. Here, we explore degradation of the eight CK1α-SACK1 (formerly known as FAM83A-H) complexes initiated by molecular glue degraders primarily designed to target Ser/Thr kinase CK1α. We demonstrate that lenalidomide-derived degraders DEG-77 and SJ3149, which selectively target the CK1α isoform, co-degrade multiple SACK1 proteins. We show that the degradation of SACK1 proteins by DEG-77 and SJ3149 requires CK1α, the CUL4ACRBN E3 ligase complex and the proteasome. In cells derived from palmoplantar keratoderma patients harbouring the CK1α-binding deficient SACK1GR265P mutation, DEG-77 targets CK1α and mitotic SACK1D but not SACK1GR265P, highlighting the requirement for CK1α-SACK1 interaction to achieve co-degradation. Our study underscores the importance of POI context in TPD and reinforces the potential for selectively targeting specific protein complexes for degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-29T13:52:59.172Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3zygrwcj4t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3zygrwcj4t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lorraine","last_name":"Glennie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicole","last_name":"Curnutt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gajanan","last_name":"Sathe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brune","last_name":"Le Chatelier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Freya ","last_name":"Goff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin-Feng","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tyrell ","last_name":"Cartwright","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karen","last_name":"Dunbar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicola","last_name":"Wood","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Macartney","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christina ","last_name":"Woo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gopal","last_name":"Sapkota","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Protein Degradation","Chemical Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rshv4pd2rt","title":"Dataset for A loop-mediated isothermal amplification assay to detect Bacteroidales and assess risk of fecal contamination","description":"We developed a rapid risk-assessment assay for fecal contamination by targeting Bacteroidales using loop-mediated isothermal amplification (LAMP). The assay allows for naked-eye observation of reactions with as few as ~8 copies of Bacteroidales per cm2 of the surface in the field. We evaluated this assay with complex field samples as well as on-site field studies. In this dataset, we've included the raw and processed data files associated with the research project. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-04T04:50:42.666Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rshv4pd2rt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rshv4pd2rt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiangshan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":"0000-0002-2620-8411"},{"first_name":"Mohsen","last_name":"Ranjbaran","orcid":"0000-0002-8577-1255"},{"first_name":"Aaron ","last_name":"Ault","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mohit","last_name":"Verma","orcid":"0000-0002-6374-3333"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Food Contamination","Rapid Diagnostics","Food Product Safety Assessment","Fresh Vegetable","Bacteroides"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fpvmmm33gd","title":"RNA-seq data for F1-F6-RiboTAC in MDA-MB-231 cells","description":"This is a supplementary dataset to a manuscript we are publishing.  This dataset includes the RNA-seq results quantifying the change in transcript abundances after treating with small molecule RNA degraders in MDA-MB-231 cells.  Please refer to our manuscript once published for the complete details.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-09T09:59:33.253Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fpvmmm33gd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fpvmmm33gd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuquan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Disney","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y742f9j8v5","title":"Three optimized assays for the evaluation of compounds that can rescue p53 mutants","description":"Drugs targeting p53 remain a proverbial Holy Grail of precision oncology, with over twenty compounds that can rescue p53 mutants reported so far. Here, we suggest three easily accessible assays to determine the thermostability, protein folding, and transcriptional activity of p53 mutants – the three go-to criteria for evaluating a rescue compound that acts by increasing p53 thermostability. Because of the diversity of p53 mutants, a compound that meets the criteria of one assay does not necessarily meet the criteria of the two other assays.\nFor complete details on the use and execution of these protocols, please refer to (Chen et al., 2021).\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-04T08:47:25.742Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y742f9j8v5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y742f9j8v5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiale","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huaxin","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhengyuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Min","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Cancer","Protein P53","Biochemistry Methods"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4n3bsmyskj","title":"Mutations in the lacZ gene","description":"This is the raw data representing 6,465 independent lacZ mutations from multiple MutaMouse experiments. We curated lacZ mutations from published studies that were collected using a positive selection assay to identify mutations in lacZ that disrupted β-gal function. These 6,465 independent lacZ mutations cause in 2,732 missense mutations that impair β-gal function. The data were used in a publication on the functional mutational landscape of the lacZ gene.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-21T10:46:44.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4n3bsmyskj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4n3bsmyskj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Meier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marc","last_name":"Beal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Angela","last_name":"Dykes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carole","last_name":"Yauk","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Iain","last_name":"Lambert","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francesco","last_name":"Marchetti","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Mutagenicity","Gene Mutation","Mutagenesis","Mutation","Mutation Rate","DNA Mutation","Mutagenesis Study","Mutagen Testing","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8b7cy856x2","title":"Supplemental Data - Novel Therapeutic Approach Targeting CXCR3 to Treat Immunotherapy Myocarditis","description":"Flow cytometry data for \"Novel Therapeutic Approach Targeting CXCR3 to Treat Immunotherapy Myocarditis\"\n- CXCR3 Blockade transwell assay\n- CD49d expression after CXCL9/10 Stimulation assay\n- Cardiomyocytes apoptosis assay\n- anti-PD1/CTLA-4 Immunotherapy Model\n- 10-02-23 in Vitro rmCXCL9 rmCXCL10\n- 9-21-23 in Vitro CXCL9 CXCL10 Blockade\n\nAutomated Quantification Script\nimagej-script.txt","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-03T02:09:55.577Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8b7cy856x2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8b7cy856x2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuhsin","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Han","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yin","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Myocarditis","Chemokine Response","Cardiology","Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yv2y66b3x5","title":"Generative AI Framework SynGlue for the Rational Design of Clinically Relevant Protein Degraders","description":"This dataset contains processed protein-level quantitative proteomics results from a TMT-multiplex LC-MS/MS study of human 22Rv1 cells under vehicle and compound-treatment conditions at 6 h and 24 h. Files include filtered protein abundance and annotations, normalized protein abundance, and differential-expression statistics. Twenty-four Thermo RAW fractions were acquired using an Orbitrap Exploris 240 platform. The vendor RAW files and original Proteome Discoverer search output are not included in this version and are being prepared for deposition in PRIDE/ProteomeXchange. A PRIDE accession will be added in a later dataset version. These CSV tables are downstream protein-level summaries and do not contain peptide-spectrum matches or the complete database-search evidence.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-22T16:45:31.827Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yv2y66b3x5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yv2y66b3x5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Saveena ","last_name":"Solanki","orcid":"0000-0003-1079-9349"},{"first_name":"Sanjay Kumar","last_name":"Mohanty","orcid":"0000-0002-1375-2223"},{"first_name":"Shiva","last_name":"Satija","orcid":"0000-0001-8768-1539"},{"first_name":"Sonam","last_name":"Chauhan","orcid":"0009-0001-3942-6696"},{"first_name":"N.V.M. Rao","last_name":"Bandaru","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sandeep","last_name":"Dukare","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nirbhay Kumar","last_name":"Tiwari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Naveen Kumar","last_name":"R","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aravind A","last_name":"B","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Subhendu","last_name":"Mukherjee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dinesh","last_name":"Chikkanna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wesley Roy","last_name":"Balasubramanian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Srinivasa Raju","last_name":"Sammeta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vishakha","last_name":"Gautam","orcid":null},{"first_name":"SAKSHI","last_name":"ARORA","orcid":"0000-0001-7535-3597"},{"first_name":"Suvendu","last_name":"Kumar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Subhadeep","last_name":"Duari","orcid":"0000-0001-9805-7635"},{"first_name":"Arushi","last_name":"Sharma","orcid":"0009-0005-2561-5903"},{"first_name":"Raidhani","last_name":"Shome","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Syed Yaseer","last_name":"Ali","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Debarka","last_name":"Sengupta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chandrasekhar","last_name":"Abbineni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Susanta","last_name":"Samajdar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gaurav","last_name":"Ahuja","orcid":"0000-0002-2837-9361"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03tjsyq23","name":"Department of Biotechnology","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/03vfp4g33","name":"Indraprastha Institute of Information Technology Delhi","grant_id":"Intramural Start-up grant"}],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6k6ng65623","title":"Generation of host-directed and virus-specific antivirals using targeted protein degradation promoted by small molecules and viral RNA mimics","description":"Targeted protein degradation (TPD), as exemplified by proteolysis-targeting chimera (PROTAC) is an emerging drug discovery platform. PROTAC molecules, which typically contain a target protein ligand linked to an E3 ligase ligand, recruit a target protein to the E3 ligase to induce its ubiquitination and degradation. Here, we applied PROTAC approaches to develop broad-spectrum antivirals targeting key host factors for many viruses and virus-specific antivirals targeting unique viral proteins.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-30T20:02:36.523Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6k6ng65623","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6k6ng65623"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nan","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Ho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fanye","last_name":"Meng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jian","last_name":"Jin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan ","last_name":"Marazzi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Antiviral"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":129485,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":142,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":129485,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":128990,"restricted":60,"embargoed":435},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":3493},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1957},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":641},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":438},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":388},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":386},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S.